                <pb facs="0754" n="628" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg628"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 M 221/120: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-04-10T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 10,
                                1925—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 10—9:15
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>150. My telegram 146 of April 9, 4 p.m. I have interviewed my French and Belgian
                    colleagues. I learn that they were also urged to present their credentials,
                    which they declined to do, the French Minister saying that his credentials had
                    not come and the Belgian Minister stating that his were addressed to former
                    President Tsao Kun. Their real reason was that Tuan’s government is only <hi
                        rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> and that it would constitute <hi
                        rendition="#italic">de jure</hi> recognition should they present their
                    credentials. They expect that when the time comes for <hi rendition="#italic">de
                        jure</hi> recognition, if it does, the powers will confer and act together.
                    The French and Belgian Ministers sent copies of their credentials to the
                    Minister of Foreign Affairs for his information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
